WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.828
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:15.263
Unit eleven: Philosophy of Science. Today you will state criteria using condition clauses.

4
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:18.366
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.405
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.669
Falsifiability.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:30.423
Falsifiability means the property of a claim that could in principle be proved wrong.

8
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:34.491
Popper treated falsifiability as the mark of real science.

9
00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:38.003
Your turn. Say: Falsifiability.

10
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.286
Falsifiability.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.411
Paradigm shift.

12
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:49.599
Paradigm shift means a fundamental change in the basic assumptions of a science.

13
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:54.354
Einstein's relativity caused a paradigm shift in physics.

14
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.055
Your turn. Say: Paradigm shift.

15
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.435
Paradigm shift.

16
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:04.463
Empiricism.

17
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.969
Empiricism means the view that knowledge comes mainly from observation and experiment.

18
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:13.558
Empiricism grounds science in what can be tested.

19
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.617
Your turn. Say: Empiricism.

20
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:20.665
Empiricism.

21
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:22.905
Scientific method.

22
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:28.308
Scientific method means a systematic way of testing ideas through evidence.

23
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:32.251
The scientific method relies on repeated, public testing.

24
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.978
Your turn. Say: Scientific method.

25
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:40.589
Scientific method.

26
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.107
Theory.

27
00:01:43.117 --> 00:01:47.345
Theory means a well-tested explanation that accounts for many observations.

28
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.449
In science a theory is not a mere guess.

29
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.400
Your turn. Say: Theory.

30
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:57.245
Theory.

31
00:01:57.983 --> 00:01:59.217
Observation.

32
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:03.575
Observation means careful watching and recording of facts.

33
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:07.634
Every observation is shaped by prior expectations.

34
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.053
Your turn. Say: Observation.

35
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.157
Observation.

36
00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:17.231
Objectivity.

37
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:22.643
Objectivity means judgment based on facts rather than personal feelings.

38
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.363
Objectivity is an ideal that communities of scientists pursue together.

39
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.695
Your turn. Say: Objectivity.

40
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:34.720
Objectivity.

41
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:36.450
Progress.

42
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.669
Progress means movement toward a better or more accurate state.

43
00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:44.038
Kuhn questioned whether science progresses steadily.

44
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.302
Your turn. Say: Progress.

45
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.272
Progress.

46
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:55.474
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.317 --> 00:03:00.360
Visal, my cousin says astrology is a science because it makes predictions.

48
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:11.660
Popper would disagree. A theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable, meaning some observation could in principle refute it.

49
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.796
But astrologers can be wrong too.

50
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:23.807
They can, yet their claims are vague enough to survive any outcome. Unless a claim risks failure, it teaches us nothing.

51
00:03:24.917 --> 00:03:27.021
So the test is the risk of being wrong?

52
00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:38.353
Precisely. Provided that a theory forbids something observable, it earns scientific status. Otherwise it merely flatters us.

53
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.291
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.554
Grammar focus: Criterion and condition clauses.

55
00:03:45.854 --> 00:03:46.995
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:51.192
A theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable.

57
00:03:52.083 --> 00:03:55.848
A claim earns status provided that it forbids something observable.

58
00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:00.108
A claim teaches us nothing unless it risks failure.

59
00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:11.660
Use 'only if', 'provided that', 'on condition that' and 'unless' to set criteria. Keep a full clause after them: only if it is falsifiable (never 'only if falsifiable' without a verb here).

60
00:04:12.417 --> 00:04:17.112
Be careful! Don't say: A theory is scientific only if it falsifiable.

61
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:22.912
Say: A theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable.

62
00:04:23.212 --> 00:04:25.038
Keep 'is' in the 'only if' clause.

63
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:28.857
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:31.028
The Mark of Science.

65
00:04:31.328 --> 00:04:37.449
Philosophy of science asks what distinguishes genuine science from pseudoscience, and how scientific knowledge grows.

66
00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:46.180
Karl Popper proposed that a theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable, that is, if some conceivable observation would show it to be false.

67
00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:55.935
Thomas Kuhn complicated this picture by arguing that science advances through paradigm shifts, in which entire frameworks are replaced rather than gradually refined.

68
00:04:56.933 --> 00:05:03.681
Critics add that observation is never entirely neutral, since scientists interpret evidence through existing theory.

69
00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:11.600
Nevertheless, scientific objectivity survives, provided that claims are exposed to public testing and open criticism.

70
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.946
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:21.350
A theory is scientific only blank it is falsifiable.

72
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.491
A, if. B, that. C, for.

73
00:05:28.033 --> 00:05:29.394
The answer is A: if.

74
00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.664
A fundamental change in a science's assumptions is a blank.

75
00:05:33.964 --> 00:05:38.326
A, paradigm shift. B, observation. C, heuristic.

76
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:44.783
The answer is A: paradigm shift.

77
00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.751
Claims that could in principle be proved wrong are blank.

78
00:05:49.051 --> 00:05:53.575
A, objective. B, falsifiable. C, empirical only.

79
00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:59.446
The answer is B: falsifiable.

80
00:06:00.583 --> 00:06:02.304
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:02.604 --> 00:06:11.517
A, Only if it falsifiable, it is science.. B, It is science only if it is falsifiable.. C, It is science only if falsifiable is..

82
00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:18.692
The answer is B: It is science only if it is falsifiable..

83
00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.812
According to the reading, objectivity survives provided that claims are…

84
00:06:24.112 --> 00:06:29.251
A, exposed to public testing. B, kept private. C, never revised.

85
00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.624
The answer is A: exposed to public testing.

86
00:06:36.833 --> 00:06:39.857
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:40.717 --> 00:06:44.910
What makes a scientific theory falsifiable, and why does that matter?

88
00:07:01.717 --> 00:07:03.089
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:33.194
A theory is falsifiable if it forbids some possible observation, so that evidence could in principle show it to be wrong. This matters because a claim that can survive any outcome explains nothing. Falsifiability forces scientists to take risks and to accept correction. Not every valuable idea is scientific, but a claim presented as science should meet this standard. Otherwise confidence replaces evidence.

90
00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.098
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:35.983 --> 00:07:41.225
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and criterion and condition clauses.

92
00:07:41.525 --> 00:07:47.718
Today's value is scientific literacy. Understand how evidence and testing build knowledge.

93
00:07:48.018 --> 00:07:50.670
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.623
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

